A component can be an ordinary value holding its HTML and the stylesheets it
needs. A const function constructs that value. Another const function combines
several parts into one page.

This is the intended `@html` component API, **not yet a shipped package
surface**. Const functions and combined stylesheet contributions still need
their planned implementation.

### Compose a documentation page

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- docs/functions/@entry.moth
    -- Proposed @html component helpers

    @html HtmlPart, html_page
    @components site_navbar, docs_sidebar, docs_topic
    @introduction content as introduction

    article #= HtmlPart(
        body = [introduction]
    )

    page #= html_page({
        site_navbar(),
        docs_sidebar("Documentation"),
        docs_topic("Functions", article)
    })

    [$in title:Functions]
    [$in description:Function declarations and calls.]

    -- Proposed multi-resource stylesheet contribution.
    [$in stylesheets:[page.stylesheets]]

    [page.body]
]

The entry owns the final document. Each component returns its body and
stylesheet references, while `html_page()` assembles the parts in order. The
page forwards those dependencies to the builder rather than letting a nested
template insert into a distant `head` or `stylesheets` slot.

A small page can skip `html_page()` and write its main content directly:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @components sidebar

    [sidebar]

    [$md:
        # Getting started

        Write an ordinary Moth program first.
    ]
]

Use the abstraction where it saves work. A component system should not make a
simple page harder to read.

### Build a component value

A sidebar can become a const function in the shared component package. Its
return type says what the page receives:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- components/sidebar.moth
    -- Proposed @html type and const function

    @html HtmlPart

    docs_sidebar #|heading String| -> HtmlPart:
        return HtmlPart(
            body = [$html:
                <aside class="docs-sidebar">
                    <h2>[heading]</h2>
                </aside>
            ],
            stylesheets = [@sidebar.css]
        )
    ;
]

A different page can call `docs_sidebar("Reference")` and receive the same
markup and the same stylesheet reference. The CSS dependency is declared once.
The returned path stays a tracked resource, not a guessed URL.

### Use a named template slot

A smaller helper can stay a template rather than become a new type:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    card #= [$html:
        <article class="card">
            <h2>[$slot title]</h2>
            [$slot]
        </article>
    ]

    [card:
        [$in title:Functions]
        Body content
    ]
]

The `title` insert fills the card's own named slot. It never searches ancestor
templates or sends a builder message. Body content lands in the default slot.

Keep using slots while a helper needs no typed result and no resource
dependency. Reach for a component value when the page needs both.

See @../../templates (Templates) for other slot and wrapper forms.
